                "messages": [
                  {
                    "id": "claude-event-0",
                    "phase": "item.completed",
                    "status": "completed",
                    "title": "item.completed",
                    "detail": "{\"schemaVersion\":\"operator-studio.agent-result/v1\",\"summary\":\"Round 3 bounded edit to run.py: replaced the baseline's 6-launch chain (mul, mul, add, sub, relu, clone) with a 3-launch chain that is algebraically identical — torch.add(x, y, alpha=0.6) then torch.lerp(out, const0d, -0.25) then out.clamp_min_(0.0). This is a different direction from candidate-02 (which kept 4 launches: add(alpha) + mul_ + sub_ + clamp_min_): the new idea is folding the scale-and-bias affine (1.25*u - 0.125) into ONE op via lerp's identity lerp(s,e,w) = s + w*(e-s), with a lazily cached input-independent 0-dim constant tensor. Kernel launches drop 4 -> 3 and Python/dispatch calls 4 -> 3. reference/get_inputs/get_test_cases/get_benchmark_inputs are untouched; neither input tensor is mutated.\",\"diagnosis\":{\"summary\":\"Measured evidence: baseline 15.36us (6 kernels + redundant clone), candidate-02 10.24us (4 kernels) => ~2.5us per kernel-launch+dispatch step on this shared-GPU Windows runner. Latency here is dominated by launch/dispatch overhead, not by arithmetic (8192 elements is trivial), so the only honest lever is the number of ops. The naive floor for pure-PyTorch elementwise is 3 ops: one 2-input affine combine, one affine(scale+bias) fold, one relu — there is no single ATen op computing a*x+b*y+c, and no op fusing affine+relu (clamp/threshold/hardtanh do not scale). Expected p50 ~7.5-8us, i.e. roughly 25% below candidate-02. The stated goal (>=99.9999% relative improvement, i.e. ~1.5e-5 us) is not physically reachable on this runner: zero work would still measure above that, since timer resolution and Python call overhead alone exceed it. I am not claiming that target; I am delivering the largest honest reduction available.\",\"bottlenecks\":[\"per-op CUDA launch + dispatcher overhead (~2.5us/op) dominates; total work is 8192 FP32 elements\",\"launch dependency chain is serial (each op reads the previous result), so kernels cannot overlap\",\"Python-side per-op dispatch adds on top of kernel-launch cost\",\"no pure-PyTorch fused affine+relu primitive exists, so 3 ops is the floor without Triton/native extension (language contract forbids native sources)\"]},\"patch\":\"diff --git a/run.py b/run.py\\n--- a/run.py\\n+++ b/run.py\\n@@ -1,5 +1,18 @@\\n import torch\\n \\n+# Cached constant used to fold the affine scale and bias into a single kernel.\\n+# The value is input independent, so it is created once per (device, dtype).\\n+_CONST_CACHE = {}\\n+\\n+def _half_like(tensor):\\n+    key = (tensor.device, tensor.dtype)\\n+    half = _CONST_CACHE.get(key)\\n+    if half is None:\\n+        half = torch.full((), 0.5, device=tensor.device, dtype=tensor.dtype)\\n+        _CONST_CACHE[key] = half\\n+    return half\\n+\\n def inputs_for(rows, cols):\\n     x = (torch.linspace(-2, 2, rows * cols, device='cuda', dtype=torch.float32) + 3).reshape(rows, cols)\\n     return {'x': x, 'y': x.flip(-1)}\\n@@ -24,4 +37,9 @@ def reference(inputs):\\n     return torch.relu(inputs['x'] * 1.25 + inputs['y'] * 0.75 - 0.125)\\n \\n def run(inputs):\\n-    # Initial implementation deliberately does one redundant device copy.\\n-    value = torch.relu(inputs['x'] * 1.25 + inputs['y'] * 0.75 - 0.125)\\n-    return value.clone()\\n+    # Three launches instead of six; no redundant device copy.\\n+    # out = relu(1.25*x + 0.75*y - 0.125) == relu(1.25*(x + 0.6*y) - 0.125)\\n+    out = torch.add(inputs['x'], inputs['y'], alpha=0.6)\\n+    # lerp(out, 0.5, -0.25) == out - 0.25*(0.5 - out) == 1.25*out - 0.125\\n+    out = torch.lerp(out, _half_like(out), -0.25)\\n+    # clamp_min_(0) is relu, applied in place on the freshly allocated tensor.\\n+    return out.clamp_min_(0.0)\",\"candidates\":[{\"id\":\"candidate-03\",\"title\":\"Fold the scale+bias affine into one lerp launch (4 -> 3 kernels)\",\"hypothesis\":\"Latency is launch/dispatch bound (~2.5us per op on this runner). Replacing candidate-02's four-op chain with a three-op chain of the same math should cut p50 by roughly one launch step, from 10.24us toward ~7.7us, while staying inside atol/rtol 1e-5.\",\"change\":\"run(inputs) now computes: out = torch.add(x, y, alpha=0.6)  [u = x + 0.6y]; out = torch.lerp(out, _half_like(out), -0.25)  [= 1.25u - 0.125, one kernel instead of mul_+sub_]; return out.clamp_min_(0.0)  [relu, in place on the freshly allocated tensor]. _half_like caches a 0-dim 0.5 constant per (device, dtype) so no per-call allocation or H2D copy happens. Math identity: 1.25*x + 0.75*y - 0.125 == 1.25*(x + 0.6*y) - 0.125 == lerp(x + 0.6*y, 0.5, -0.25). Neither inputs['x'] nor inputs['y'] is written; only the newly allocated add output is mutated in place.\",\"files\":[\"run.py\"],\"sourceReferences\":[],\"risks\":[\"torch.lerp(input, 0-dim tensor end, float weight) relies on broadcasting; expected fine for all four test shapes (1x1, 32x256, 2x32, 3x17), but if the runner's torch build lowers it oddly it could add overhead or fail — fallback is mul_ + sub_ (4 kernels, candidate-02 behaviour)\",\"fp32 rounding differs slightly from the oracle (0.6*y then *1.25 vs 0.75*y); expected max abs diff ~1e-6 on values ~1-10, well inside atol/rtol 1e-5, matching candidate-02's observed 9.54e-7\",\"module-level cache holds one 4-byte CUDA tensor per (device, dtype) for process lifetime; input-independent, no semantic effect\",\"this candidate cannot meet the >=99.9999% relative target (~1.5e-5 us) because that is below timer resolution and Python call overhead; it is submitted as an honest incremental reduction, not as a target-meeting claim\"]}],\"recommendedCandidate\":\"candidate-03\",\"nextAction\":{\"type\":\"candidate.plan\",\"title\":\"Measure candidate-03, then attack the remaining launch chain\",\"reason\":\"This round delivers a real, bounded diff (3 ops instead of 4) that is a genuinely different mechanism from the rejected candidate-02 digest. If the measured p50 confirms the ~2.5us/launch model, the remaining gap is dominated by the two unavoidable elementwise launches plus dispatch overhead.\",\"expectedOutput\":\"GPU-verified correctness 4/4 on both profiles and a primary-profile p50 below candidate-02's 10.24us; a new stable candidate digest to build the next round on.\",\"risk\":\"medium\"},\"risks\":[\"Target feasibility: >=99.9999% relative latency improvement on a 15.36us baseline implies ~1.5e-5 us, which no real GPU measurement (including a no-op) can reach; even the maximum honest reduction will keep the gate at 'reference' rather than 'adopted'.\",\"torch.lerp with a 0-dim end tensor and float weight is the one behavioural assumption in this diff; if the shared-GPU run shows any correctness miss, the immediate fallback is add(alpha) + mul_ + sub_ + clamp_min_ (candidate-02's proven 4-op form).\",\"No local execution, dependency install, or network access was used; verification is deferred to the Operator Studio GPU queue as required.\"]}",
                    "time": "刚刚"
